/* Historia — content (English): The Grand Siècle */
HISTORIA_CONTENT["grand-siecle"] = {
  intro: "From the Edict of Nantes to the death of Louis XIV: a century of building kings, iron cardinals, endless wars and a monarch who chose the sun as his emblem. Step into the age of Versailles, Molière and Vauban.",
  cast: [
    {"n":"Henry IV","r":"King of France and Navarre","d":"1553 – 1610","b":"A Protestant who became Catholic to reconcile a torn kingdom, he signed the Edict of Nantes and rebuilt the country with Sully. Nicknamed the “Vert Galant”, he remained the “good King Henry” until Ravaillac's knife."},
    {"n":"Cardinal de Richelieu","r":"Chief minister of Louis XIII","d":"1585 – 1642","b":"A bishop turned cardinal, he strengthened the king's authority, broke the military power of the Protestants at La Rochelle and founded the French Academy. Feared or admired, he was known as the “Red Eminence”."},
    {"n":"Louis XIV","r":"The Sun King","d":"1638 – 1715","b":"King at four, he governed alone from 1661, made Versailles the centre of the kingdom and reigned seventy-two years, the longest reign in French history. He shone over Europe, but left an exhausted kingdom."},
    {"n":"Molière","r":"Author and actor of comedies","d":"1622 – 1673","b":"The son of an upholsterer turned actor, he made the court laugh with The Miser, Tartuffe and The Imaginary Invalid. Taken ill on stage in 1673, he died a few hours later. People still say “the language of Molière”."},
    {"n":"Madame de Maintenon","r":"Governess then secret wife of the king","d":"1635 – 1719","b":"Granddaughter of a Protestant poet, raised in hardship, widow of the poet Scarron, she became governess of the king's children. Louis XIV married her in secret, and she founded Saint-Cyr to educate noble girls without fortune."}
  ],
  levels: [
    {
      title: "Henry IV, the King of Peace",
      lesson: {"d":"1589 – 1610","t":"Henry IV, the King Who Reconciles the Kingdom","hook":"Imagine: it is 1593 and you stand before the gates of Paris, and the king of France is not allowed in. He is Protestant, the city is Catholic, and it has shut its gates to him. How do you enter your own capital?","p":"Henry is born at Pau in December 1553, in the small kingdom of Navarre, at the foot of the Pyrenees. His mother, Joan of Albret, raises him in the Protestant religion, and he grows up in the midst of the Wars of Religion, which have torn the kingdom apart since 1562. In August 1572, he marries Marguerite of Valois, sister of King Charles IX. A few days later, the wedding turns into a nightmare: the Saint Bartholomew massacre. Henry's life is spared, but only by agreeing, under threat, to become Catholic. He will return to the Protestant faith on leaving the court a few years later.\n\n## A King Without a Capital\n\nHere is the problem. In 1589, Henry III, the last of the Valois, is stabbed by the monk Jacques Clément. His nearest heir is Henry of Navarre, first of the Bourbons. But most French people are Catholic, and the League, an uncompromising Catholic party backed by Spain, refuses a Protestant king. For four years, Henry must therefore conquer his kingdom at sword's point. He wins at Arques, then at Ivry, where he is said to have told his men to follow his white plume. Paris, however, starving but obstinate, keeps its gates shut.\n\nSo Henry decides. In July 1593, at Saint-Denis, he abjures Protestantism. He is credited with the line “Paris is well worth a Mass”, probably invented after the fact, but which sums up the situation: he chooses the peace of the kingdom over loyalty to his origins. Many Protestants feel betrayed, many Catholics remain wary. As Reims is in the hands of the League, he is crowned at Chartres in February 1594, and on 22 March he enters Paris, almost without a fight.\n\n## Making Two Religions Live Together\n\nThe last fires still had to be put out. At the end of the 1590s, two texts change everything. The treaty of Vervins sends the Spaniards home. And at Nantes, an edict organises what seemed impossible: two religions in one kingdom. Protestants obtain freedom of conscience, the right to hold worship in specified places, access to public office and places of security, fortified towns where they can protect themselves. Catholics keep their dominant place, and the king remains Catholic. It is not yet modern tolerance, rather a coexistence wrung out of weariness. But after decades of massacres, it is already a feat.\n\nThe kingdom is ruined, the countryside ravaged. Henry relies on Maximilien de Béthune, duke of Sully, an old Protestant companion in arms, named superintendent of finances. Sully puts the accounts in order and repays part of the debts. He is credited with the line “Ploughing and pasture are the two breasts of France”. The king also supports the agronomist Olivier de Serres, whose “Théâtre d'agriculture” teaches peasants how to drain, plant and tend the land, and he encourages the growing of mulberry trees to raise silkworms. Another line is credited to him: that every peasant might put “a chicken in the pot” on Sunday.\n\nParis, for its part, becomes a building site. The Pont-Neuf, completed in 1607, is the first bridge in the capital without houses on its sides: at last you can discover the Seine there, and people crowd along it among onlookers, singers and charlatans. The Place Royale, which we call Place des Vosges, lines up its pavilions of red brick and white stone around a perfect square. In 1600, Henry marries Marie de' Medici, who will give him the future Louis XIII. The French call him the “Vert Galant” for his many affairs, and the “good King Henry” for his good nature.\n\n## Rue de la Ferronnerie\n\nOn 13 May 1610, Marie de' Medici is crowned at Saint-Denis. The next day, Henry, who is preparing a military campaign, gets into his carriage. In rue de la Ferronnerie, a narrow street, the vehicle is blocked by carts. A man leaps onto the running board: François Ravaillac, a former schoolmaster, a fanatic convinced that the king is about to make war on the pope. He strikes with his knife, and Henry IV dies almost at once. The country is seized with horror: the new king is eight, and everything his father had built suddenly seems fragile.","fun":["Even today, the Pont-Neuf is the oldest bridge in Paris, and its name has never made it any younger. At the time, it was a place of spectacle: tooth-pullers, remedy sellers and singers set up there in the open air.","The song “Vive Henri IV” seems old, but actually dates from the 18th century. It served as a royal anthem under the Restoration, a sign that the memory of the good King Henry had remained very popular."]},
      items: [
        { type: "qcm", q: "What status does the Edict of Nantes grant the Protestants of the kingdom?", c: "Freedom of conscience and worship in specified places", w: ["The status of official religion of the kingdom", "A ban on holding any public office", "An obligation to leave France within three months"], e: "Signed in April 1598, the edict recognises freedom of conscience, public worship in many places and places of security. Catholicism nevertheless remains the king's religion." },
        { type: "qcm", q: "Which Protestant minister, superintendent of finances, helps Henry IV restore the kingdom?", c: "Sully", w: ["Richelieu", "Colbert", "Mazarin"], e: "Maximilien de Béthune, duke of Sully, repays part of the debts and supports agriculture and roads. The other three ministers come later in the century." },
        { type: "qcm", q: "Which religion does Henry IV embrace in 1593 to be accepted by most French people?", c: "Catholicism", w: ["Lutheranism", "Anglicanism", "Orthodoxy"], e: "Raised Protestant, Henry abjures at Saint-Denis. This conversion gradually opens to him the gates of Paris and the coronation of Chartres, in 1594." },
        { type: "qcm", q: "Which man stabs Henry IV in his carriage, in rue de la Ferronnerie?", c: "François Ravaillac", w: ["Jacques Clément", "Jean Châtel", "Balthasar Gérard"], e: "Jacques Clément had killed Henry III in 1589, Jean Châtel had wounded Henry IV in 1594. Ravaillac, a former schoolmaster, acted alone and was executed." },
        { type: "qcm", q: "Which Paris bridge, completed under Henry IV, is today the oldest in the capital?", c: "The Pont-Neuf", w: ["The Pont Marie", "The Pont-Royal", "The Pont des Arts"], e: "Despite its name, the Pont-Neuf, finished in 1607, is the oldest bridge in Paris still standing. It was one of the first without houses on its sides." },
        { type: "tf", q: "Henry IV is the first king of the Bourbon dynasty.", a: true, e: "A descendant of Saint Louis through a younger branch, he succeeds Henry III, the last Valois. The Bourbons will reign until 1830, with the interruption of the Revolution and the Empire." },
        { type: "tf", q: "The Edict of Nantes forces all the king's subjects to become Catholic.", a: false, e: "It is the opposite: the edict organises the coexistence of two religions. Only in 1685, under Louis XIV, would this tolerance be withdrawn from the Protestants." },
        { type: "order", q: "Put these moments in Henry IV's life in chronological order.", items: [{ t: "Marriage to Marguerite of Valois", y: "1572" }, { t: "He becomes king at Henry III's death", y: "1589" }, { t: "He abjures Protestantism", y: "1593" }, { t: "He is assassinated by Ravaillac", y: "1610" }] },
        { type: "match", q: "Match each name to its role or place.", pairs: [["Marie de' Medici", "The king's second wife (1600)"], ["Olivier de Serres", "Author of the “Théâtre d'agriculture”"], ["Place Royale", "Future Place des Vosges"], ["Vervins", "Peace treaty with Spain"]] },
        { type: "year", q: "In which year does Henry IV sign the Edict of Nantes?", y: 1598, min: 1540, max: 1640, tol: 1, e: "The edict is signed at Nantes in April 1598, the same year as the peace of Vervins with Spain. The Wars of Religion, begun in 1562, thus come to an end." }
      ]
    },
    {
      title: "A Regent, a Child King",
      lesson: {"d":"1610 – 1624","t":"An Italian Regent and a Young King","hook":"Imagine: you are eight, your father the king has just been assassinated, and your mother governs surrounded by Italians the people hate. You wait for your hour. For seven years, this is your life.","p":"Henry IV is dead. His son Louis XIII, born at Fontainebleau in September 1601, is not yet nine. The law says the king comes of age at thirteen, and in the meantime it is his mother, Marie de' Medici, who receives the regency: the day after the tragedy, the Parlement of Paris entrusts it to her. Born in Florence, daughter of the grand duke of Tuscany, she keeps around her a circle from Italy, which does nothing for her popularity. She quickly dismisses Sully, Henry IV's minister, and spends in pensions the treasure he had built up.\n\n## The Italian Favourites\n\nHer great confidants are Leonora Galigaï, a childhood friend from Florence, and her husband, Concino Concini. Concini buys offices, becomes marquis d'Ancre then marshal, and amasses a fortune. The people and the great lords hate him: he is a foreigner, an upstart, and he makes no secret of it. The princes revolt several times, and the regent buys their calm with money.\n\nTo soothe the kingdom, the Estates General are summoned in Paris, the assembly of the nobility, the clergy and the third estate. In the autumn of 1614, the deputies talk for months, quarrel over privileges, offices and pensions, then part with nothing decided. No one knows it yet, but it is the last time before the Revolution: they will not be seen again until 1789. The following year, the regent marries her son, fourteen, to the Spanish infanta Anne of Austria, of the same age, while her daughter Elisabeth marries the heir to Spain. The two princesses are exchanged at the border, on the Bidassoa.\n\n## A Child Who Watches and Waits\n\nMeanwhile, Louis grows up. His physician Jean Héroard keeps day after day a meticulous journal of his life: what he eats, how he sleeps, what he says, his nightmares. It is a mine for understanding the childhood of a 17th-century king. Louis loves hunting, dogs, music, and is bored in councils. He is declared of age at thirteen, but his mother keeps governing. At fifteen, he understands that he is king in name only.\n\nOn 24 April 1617, the coup de théâtre comes. On the king's orders, the captain of the guards Vitry waits for Concini at the entrance to the Louvre, and cuts him down on the drawbridge. Louis XIII, it is said, then declares that from this hour he is king. Leonora Galigaï is arrested, tried for witchcraft and executed. Marie de' Medici is sent away to Blois, under watch. The young king turns to a new favourite, Charles d'Albert de Luynes, who had won his trust through a shared passion for hunting and birds of prey.\n\nMarie de' Medici does not resign herself. One night in February 1619, she escapes from the château of Blois by a window and joins her supporters. A war between mother and son threatens. A young bishop of Luçon, Armand du Plessis de Richelieu, who had followed the queen into exile, helps negotiate the reconciliation. The name of this bishop will soon count.\n\n## A Florentine Palace in Paris\n\nMarie remains a great builder. She has the Luxembourg palace raised, whose plan is inspired by the Pitti palace of her Florentine childhood, and whose gardens are today one of Parisians' favourite places to stroll. She also commissions from Rubens a series of twenty-four great paintings that tell her life, mixing real people and ancient gods. They are now displayed at the Louvre, in a room devoted to them.","fun":["The Luxembourg palace, where the Senate sits today, owes its name to the duke of Luxembourg, whose mansion and land Marie de' Medici had bought to build her residence.","Jean Héroard's journal runs to thousands of pages and notes even the meals, games and moods of little Louis. Thanks to it, we know better how a child king lived than many adults of his time."]},
      items: [
        { type: "qcm", q: "Who holds the regency of the kingdom after Henry IV's death?", c: "Marie de' Medici", w: ["Anne of Austria", "Catherine de' Medici", "Marguerite of Valois"], e: "Marie de' Medici governs in the name of her son Louis XIII. Anne of Austria, Marie's daughter-in-law, would in turn be regent, but only from 1643." },
        { type: "qcm", q: "How old is Louis XIII when his father is assassinated?", c: "Eight", w: ["Thirteen", "Fifteen", "Eighteen"], e: "Born in 1601, Louis becomes king as a child. Royal majority is set at thirteen, but he lets his mother govern until the coup of 1617." },
        { type: "qcm", q: "Which Italian favourite of the regent is cut down at the Louvre in 1617 on the young king's orders?", c: "Concino Concini", w: ["Charles d'Albert de Luynes", "Jules Mazarin", "Maximilien de Sully"], e: "Having become marshal d'Ancre, Concini is killed by Vitry, captain of the guards. His wife will be executed soon after, accused of witchcraft, and Luynes takes his place at the king's side." },
        { type: "qcm", q: "Which Parisian palace does Marie de' Medici have built from 1615, in memory of Florence?", c: "The Luxembourg palace", w: ["The Palais-Royal", "The Tuileries palace", "The town hall"], e: "She takes inspiration from the Pitti palace of her native city. The Palais-Royal would be built by Richelieu, and the Tuileries had been by another Medici, Catherine." },
        { type: "qcm", q: "Which great Flemish painter makes twenty-four paintings of her life for Marie de' Medici?", c: "Peter Paul Rubens", w: ["Rembrandt", "Velázquez", "Anthony van Dyck"], e: "Painted around 1622-1625 for the Luxembourg palace, these huge paintings glorify the queen. You can see them today at the Louvre." },
        { type: "tf", q: "In 1615, Louis XIII marries the Spanish infanta Anne of Austria.", a: true, e: "This marriage, decided by his mother, draws France closer to Catholic Spain. It will give birth, in 1638, to the future Louis XIV." },
        { type: "tf", q: "Marie de' Medici is a Spanish princess.", a: false, e: "She was born in Florence in 1575, daughter of the grand duke of Tuscany. The Spanish infanta is her daughter-in-law Anne of Austria, wife of Louis XIII." },
        { type: "order", q: "Put these moments of the regency in chronological order.", items: [{ t: "Birth of Louis XIII", y: "1601" }, { t: "Regency of Marie de' Medici", y: "1610" }, { t: "Marriage to Anne of Austria", y: "1615" }, { t: "Assassination of Concini", y: "1617" }, { t: "Marie de' Medici escapes from Blois", y: "1619" }] },
        { type: "match", q: "Match each figure to his or her role.", pairs: [["Anne of Austria", "Infanta of Spain, wife of Louis XIII"], ["Duke of Luynes", "Favourite of the young Louis XIII"], ["Vitry", "Captain of the guards who killed Concini"], ["Blois", "Town where the queen mother is exiled"]] },
        { type: "year", q: "In which year are the last Estates General before the French Revolution held?", y: 1614, min: 1550, max: 1650, tol: 1, e: "Gathered in Paris from October 1614 to February 1615, they achieve nothing. The king will summon them no more: it will take until 1789." }
      ]
    },
    {
      title: "Richelieu, the Man in Red",
      lesson: {"d":"1624 – 1642","t":"Richelieu, the Cardinal Who Built the State","hook":"A cardinal dressed in red, surrounded by cats, holds half of Europe in check from his desk. Should he be feared or admired? His contemporaries never agreed.","p":"Armand du Plessis is born in Paris in 1585, into a family of minor Poitou nobility. He was meant for arms, but his elder brother gives up the bishopric of Luçon, which the family wants to keep: Armand takes his place, studies theology and becomes a bishop very young. He makes himself noticed at the Estates General of 1614 with a brilliant speech, follows the queen mother into disgrace, then returns to court. A cardinal in 1622, he enters the King's Council in 1624, and will never leave it.\n\n## The King, the Queen Mother and the Cardinal\n\nLouis XIII is a wary, shy but stubborn king. Richelieu understands that he must first win and keep his trust. His enemies are powerful, starting with the queen mother, who had launched him and who turns against him. In November 1630, the Day of the Dupes enters history. Marie de' Medici demands the cardinal's dismissal and thinks she has won. Richelieu, who thought himself lost, joins Louis XIII at the little hunting lodge of Versailles. The king renews his trust in him, and it is the queen mother who goes into exile. The “dupes” are those who had believed in the cardinal's disgrace.\n\nWhat does Richelieu want? A king obeyed throughout the kingdom. He sends to the provinces intendants, the king's agents in charge of justice, police and finance, who trim the power of the governors. He has the castles of rebellious lords razed. He bans duelling, and Count Montmorency-Bouteville, who fought in broad daylight on the Place Royale, is beheaded. Conspirators are treated without pity, which earns him a reputation as a ruthless man. He also develops the navy, convinced that France must be a sea power.\n\n## The Siege of La Rochelle\n\nBut the great challenge is the Protestants. Since the Edict of Nantes, they have fortified towns that form almost a state within the state. The most powerful is La Rochelle, a prosperous Atlantic port, which calls on England for help. Richelieu accompanies the king's army and chooses a radical method: cut the town off from the sea. He has a dyke more than a kilometre long built across the roadstead, while cannon and ships forbid access. The English fleet tries to force the passage and fails. In the town, the mayor Jean Guiton resists, but hunger sets in and the population collapses. After more than a year, La Rochelle surrenders.\n\nThen comes the peace of Alès. It leaves the Protestants freedom of worship, but abolishes their places of security. Richelieu did not want to persecute a religion: he wanted to end a military power. Abroad, moreover, he remains the ally of Protestant princes against the Habsburgs. This is his style: the interest of the state above all, what will be called reason of state.\n\n## A Minister Who Loves Words\n\nThe cardinal is also a man of culture. He has the Palais-Cardinal built in Paris, which he will bequeath to the king: it is today's Palais-Royal. He rebuilds the Sorbonne, whose chapel houses his tomb, and creates in Poitou a model town that still bears his name. He loves the theatre, protects poets and writes plays himself. In 1631, he backs Théophraste Renaudot, a physician who launches La Gazette, ancestor of the French press. In 1635, he gives official form to the French Academy, a small circle of men of letters who already met at one of their homes, charged with fixing the language. Its forty members are nicknamed the Immortals.\n\nTwo nicknames go with him. The people speak of the “Red Eminence”, because of his cardinal's robe. His closest adviser, the Capuchin François Leclerc du Tremblay, called Father Joseph, wears a grey robe: he is the “Grey Eminence”, an expression that has passed into the language to describe one who advises from the shadows. Richelieu dies in December 1642, worn out by illness. On his deathbed, he recommends Mazarin to the king as his successor.","fun":["Richelieu adored cats, and he had many in his apartments at the Palais-Cardinal. Something to soften the image of a minister whom his enemies described as a heartless tyrant.","Richelieu's Palais-Royal today houses the Council of State, the Constitutional Council and the Ministry of Culture. Its garden is open to all: you can stroll where the cardinal dreamed of a strong state."]},
      items: [
        { type: "qcm", q: "Of which king is Cardinal de Richelieu the chief minister?", c: "Louis XIII", w: ["Henry IV", "Louis XIV", "Louis XV"], e: "Richelieu serves Louis XIII from 1624 to 1642. The king survives him by only a few months: he dies in May 1643." },
        { type: "qcm", q: "Which great Protestant stronghold does Richelieu besiege in 1627-1628?", c: "La Rochelle", w: ["Montpellier", "Sedan", "Grenoble"], e: "Supported in vain by an English fleet, La Rochelle is blockaded by land and sea. Its fall deprives the Protestants of their main military bastion." },
        { type: "qcm", q: "Which institution does Richelieu found to watch over the French language?", c: "The French Academy", w: ["The Collège de France", "The Sorbonne", "The Academy of Sciences"], e: "Its forty members, nicknamed the “Immortals”, must compile a dictionary. The Collège de France dated from Francis I, the Academy of Sciences would come under Colbert." },
        { type: "qcm", q: "How does Richelieu prevent English ships from supplying La Rochelle?", c: "By barring the harbour entrance with a long dyke", w: ["By having a canal dug around the town", "By burning the English fleet at anchor", "By buying the English withdrawal"], e: "The dyke, over a kilometre long, closes the roadstead. Deprived of relief, the starving town finally surrenders in October 1628." },
        { type: "qcm", q: "What do the Protestants lose by the peace of Alès, signed in 1629?", c: "Their places of security", w: ["Their freedom of worship", "The right to own land", "The right to marry"], e: "They keep the freedom of worship guaranteed by the Edict of Nantes, but no longer have fortresses. Their political and military power is broken." },
        { type: "tf", q: "The French Academy, founded under Richelieu, still exists today.", a: true, e: "It sits at the Institut de France, in Paris, and continues to receive new elected members. It has outlived kings, the Revolution and the regimes that followed." },
        { type: "tf", q: "Richelieu was the chief minister of Louis XIV.", a: false, e: "Richelieu dies in 1642, a year before Louis XIII, when Louis XIV, born in 1638, is only a child. It is Mazarin who succeeds him." },
        { type: "order", q: "Put these stages of Richelieu's life in chronological order.", items: [{ t: "He becomes a cardinal", y: "1622" }, { t: "He enters the King's Council", y: "1624" }, { t: "The Day of the Dupes", y: "1630" }, { t: "Birth of La Gazette", y: "1631" }, { t: "Death of Richelieu", y: "1642" }] },
        { type: "match", q: "Match each name to what relates to Richelieu.", pairs: [["Palais-Cardinal", "Became the Palais-Royal"], ["Théophraste Renaudot", "Physician and journalist"], ["Intendants", "King's agents in the provinces"], ["Marie de' Medici", "Queen mother set aside in 1630"]] },
        { type: "year", q: "In which year does La Rochelle surrender to the king, after a long siege?", y: 1628, min: 1570, max: 1660, tol: 1, e: "The town capitulates on 28 October 1628, after more than a year of siege and famine. Its population, weakened by hunger, has fallen sharply." }
      ]
    },
    {
      title: "Thirty Years of Iron and Fire",
      lesson: {"d":"1618 – 1648","t":"The Thirty Years' War","hook":"May 1618, Prague castle: furious nobles seize two envoys of the emperor and throw them out of the window. They survive the fall. Yet this gesture will cost millions of people their lives.","p":"To understand, you have to look at the map. The Holy Roman Empire is a mosaic of hundreds of principalities, free cities and bishoprics, more or less loyal to an emperor of the house of Habsburg, who also reigns in Vienna. Since the Reformation, some princes are Catholic, others Lutheran or Calvinist, and the peace of Augsburg of 1555 settled the question only for a time. In Bohemia, Protestant nobles worry about the policy of Ferdinand II, a Catholic Habsburg, future emperor, determined to restore his religion. On 23 May 1618, they throw two imperial representatives out of a window of Prague castle.\n\n## A War That Never Ends\n\nWhat was only a local revolt becomes a European war. Bohemia is crushed in 1620 at the White Mountain, but the Protestants find allies. Denmark intervenes, then Sweden under Gustavus Adolphus, a brilliant war leader who lands in Germany in 1630. He wins great victories, then is killed at Lützen in 1632, in the middle of a won battle. On the imperial side, Wallenstein, a Bohemian lord enriched by the war, who keeps his own army, is assassinated in 1634 by his own officers.\n\nHow do people live during this conflict? Badly. Armies are walking towns: soldiers, mercenaries, women, children, merchants, mules. They cannot be fed, so they live off the land, take the grain, the cattle, the money, and woe to whoever resists. To this are added plague and hunger. In 1631, Magdeburg, a great Protestant city on the Elbe, is taken by imperial troops and ravaged by fire. Whole regions lose a large part of their inhabitants, and it will take generations to recover the earlier level.\n\nThe Lorraine engraver Jacques Callot, born at Nancy, left a series of etchings, “The Miseries of War”: soldiers plundering a farm, burned villages, hanged men strung from trees. Three centuries later, Bertolt Brecht would set in this war his play “Mother Courage and Her Children”, the story of a trader who follows the armies.\n\n## France Enters the Stage\n\nFrance is Catholic, but it is encircled by Habsburg territories: Spain to the south, the Spanish Netherlands to the north, the Empire to the east. Richelieu judges this encirclement dangerous. For years, he discreetly helps their adversaries with money: Sweden, the United Provinces, German princes. Then, in 1635, he openly declares war on Spain. The beginnings are hard: in 1636, the Spaniards take Corbie, a few days' march from Paris, which panics before the enemy is pushed back.\n\nOn 19 May 1643, five days after the death of Louis XIII, a Spanish army besieges Rocroi, in the Ardennes, thinking to profit from the regency of a four-year-old king. The duke of Enghien, twenty-one, the future Great Condé, breaks it. The redoubtable tercios, those Spanish infantry squares said to be invincible, hold out to the end and are annihilated. Spain's prestige is durably dented.\n\n## Westphalia, the Peace of the Exhausted\n\nThe negotiations are held in two cities of Westphalia, Münster, Catholic, and Osnabrück, Protestant: each camp has its town. The talks last years, while the armies keep fighting. In 1648, the treaties are signed. German princes obtain broad autonomy, the United Provinces and Switzerland are recognised as independent, and France receives rights over Alsace. The Empire remains fragmented into hundreds of states. France and Spain, for their part, will fight eleven more years.","fun":["The defenestration of 1618 was not Prague's first: in 1419, supporters of the reformer Jan Hus had already thrown town councillors out of the town hall window. The city had a certain taste for this method.","The name “Peace of Westphalia” remains attached to the idea of sovereign states dealing with one another as equals. Historians still speak of a “Westphalian order” to describe the Europe born of these treaties."]},
      items: [
        { type: "qcm", q: "Which event in Prague triggers the Thirty Years' War?", c: "The defenestration of two representatives of the emperor", w: ["The execution of the king of Bohemia", "The siege of Vienna by the Turks", "The battle of the White Mountain"], e: "The rebel Protestant nobles throw the imperial envoys from the top of the castle. The White Mountain, in 1620, is a later battle, where the Habsburgs crush the Bohemians." },
        { type: "qcm", q: "Which king of Sweden intervenes in Germany to support the Protestants and dies at Lützen in 1632?", c: "Gustavus Adolphus", w: ["Charles XII", "Christian IV of Denmark", "Frederick V of the Palatinate"], e: "Considered a great war leader, he is killed in the midst of victory. Charles XII would reign only half a century later, and Christian IV was king of Denmark." },
        { type: "qcm", q: "Which victory of 1643 lets the young duke of Enghien, future Great Condé, defeat the Spanish infantry?", c: "Rocroi", w: ["Lens", "Fontenoy", "Fleurus"], e: "At twenty-one, Condé breaks the old Spanish tercios reputed invincible. Lens, another victory of Condé, dates from 1648." },
        { type: "qcm", q: "Which region does France obtain partly thanks to the treaties of Westphalia?", c: "Alsace", w: ["Lorraine", "Franche-Comté", "Roussillon"], e: "It receives rights over a large part of Alsace. Lorraine would be joined only in 1766, Franche-Comté in 1678 and Roussillon in 1659." },
        { type: "qcm", q: "Which great German Protestant city is sacked in 1631 by imperial troops?", c: "Magdeburg", w: ["Nuremberg", "Cologne", "Dresden"], e: "Fire and massacre destroy most of the city. This tragedy becomes for Protestants the symbol of the horrors of war." },
        { type: "tf", q: "Richelieu's Catholic France supported Protestant powers against the Habsburgs.", a: true, e: "Richelieu reasons in terms of the interest of the state more than of religion. He subsidises Sweden and the United Provinces before entering the war himself." },
        { type: "tf", q: "The Thirty Years' War takes place mostly on French soil.", a: false, e: "Its main battlefield is the Holy Roman Empire, in Germany and Bohemia, where some regions lose a large part of their population." },
        { type: "order", q: "Put these episodes of the Thirty Years' War in chronological order.", items: [{ t: "Start of the Bohemian revolt", y: "1618" }, { t: "Sack of Magdeburg", y: "1631" }, { t: "France openly enters the war", y: "1635" }, { t: "Victory of Rocroi", y: "1643" }] },
        { type: "match", q: "Match each name to its role in the Thirty Years' War.", pairs: [["Ferdinand II", "Catholic emperor of the Empire"], ["Jacques Callot", "Engraver of the “Miseries of War”"], ["Wallenstein", "Imperial generalissimo, killed in 1634"], ["Münster and Osnabrück", "Towns of the 1648 negotiations"]] },
        { type: "year", q: "In which year are the treaties of Westphalia signed?", y: 1648, min: 1580, max: 1680, tol: 1, e: "Signed at Münster and Osnabrück, they recognise the independence of the United Provinces and redraw the balance of Europe. The Empire remains fragmented into hundreds of states." }
      ]
    },
    {
      title: "Mazarin and the Fronde",
      lesson: {"d":"1643 – 1661","t":"Mazarin, the Fronde and Peace","hook":"August 1648: in one night, Paris is covered with barricades. At the Palais-Royal, a nine-year-old child, the king, hears the anger of his people rumble beneath his windows. What will he do when he is grown?","p":"On 14 May 1643, Louis XIII dies. His son, born on 5 September 1638 after twenty-three years of childless marriage, was given the name Louis-Dieudonné, “given by God”. He is four. His mother, Anne of Austria, becomes regent, and surprises everyone: instead of dismissing Cardinal Mazarin, a former collaborator of Richelieu, she entrusts the government to him.\n\nGiulio Mazarini was born in the Abruzzi, in Italy, trained with the Jesuits in Rome and served the pope as a diplomat. Naturalised French, a cardinal without ever having been ordained a priest, he is skilful, supple, patient. He is also very unpopular: a foreigner, greedy for money, accused of getting rich while the people grow poor.\n\n## Barricades in Paris\n\nThe war against Spain is costly. To finance it, Mazarin multiplies taxes, loans and sales of offices, and the magistrates of the Parlement of Paris, who had to register the edicts, grow worried. On 26 August 1648, the regent has the councillor Broussel arrested, a popular magistrate. Paris flares up: within hours, the city bristles with barricades, chains are stretched across the streets. Cardinal de Retz, coadjutor of the archbishop of Paris, scheming and brilliant, joins the agitation. Anne of Austria gives way and frees Broussel.\n\nAccording to the most common explanation, the name Fronde comes from a children's game: the urchins of Paris fought with stones thrown with a sling in the town's ditches, and ran off when the police came. The opponents of power call themselves frondeurs, like urchins defying authority. The nickname, at first mocking, becomes a banner: fashions “à la Fronde” are launched and thousands of pamphlets against Mazarin, the mazarinades, are printed.\n\n## The Court Flees\n\nIn the night of 5 to 6 January 1649, the eve of Epiphany, the court secretly leaves Paris for Saint-Germain-en-Laye: the little king, his mother, Mazarin. The royal army, with the prince of Condé at its head, besieges the capital. A shaky peace is concluded in spring, but in 1650 Mazarin has Condé arrested, who had become too cumbersome. The Fronde becomes that of the princes: the viscount of Turenne, a great military man, goes from one camp to another; Condé, freed, takes up arms against the king and even allies with Spain.\n\nIn 1651, at thirteen, Louis is declared of age. Mazarin goes into exile twice to calm tempers, but governs from afar. In July 1652, in a street fight in the faubourg Saint-Antoine, Condé's army is pinned against the walls of Paris. The Grande Mademoiselle, Anne-Marie-Louise of Orléans, the king's cousin, has the gates of the city opened and the Bastille cannon fired at the royal troops, which saves Condé.\n\n## The Return of Order\n\nTired of disorder, the Parisians soon call the king back, who returns to the capital in October 1652; Mazarin returns in February 1653. The Fronde is over, and Louis XIV will keep a lasting distrust of Paris, the Parlements and the great lords. Mazarin can then end the war: the treaty of the Pyrenees is negotiated on Pheasant Island, in the middle of the Bidassoa. Spain cedes Roussillon and Artois. In 1660, Louis marries at Saint-Jean-de-Luz his cousin, the infanta Maria Theresa. He had loved Marie Mancini, the cardinal's niece, but Mazarin imposes on him a marriage of reason of state. He dies in March 1661 and leaves his rich library, open to readers: the Mazarine library, the oldest public library in France.","fun":["Even today, we say of a person who readily challenges authority that he has a “frondeur” spirit. The word has outlived the revolt that gave birth to it.","The Mazarine library still exists: it occupies a building on the quai de Conti, in Paris, under the dome of the Institut de France, and keeps the books gathered by the cardinal."]},
      items: [
        { type: "qcm", q: "Which cardinal governs France in the name of Anne of Austria and the young Louis XIV?", c: "Jules Mazarin", w: ["Richelieu", "Jean-Baptiste Colbert", "Michel Le Tellier"], e: "Giulio Mazzarini, born in the Abruzzi, was naturalised French in 1639. He made his career with Richelieu before succeeding him." },
        { type: "qcm", q: "According to the most common explanation, where does the name “Fronde” come from?", c: "From a throwing weapon the urchins of Paris played with", w: ["From the name of a revolt leader, the count of Fronde", "From a rallying cry of the capital's merchants", "From a Latin word meaning “revolt”"], e: "Children of Paris threw stones with slings in the ditches, despite bans. The word, mocking, designates those who oppose the authorities." },
        { type: "qcm", q: "Which prince of the blood, a great war leader, spends time in the Frondeurs' camp then in that of Spain?", c: "The Great Condé", w: ["Marshal de Villars", "Gaston of Orléans", "The duke of Vendôme"], e: "Arrested in 1650 on Mazarin's orders, Condé takes up arms against the king and even joins the Spanish army. He will return to favour at the treaty of the Pyrenees." },
        { type: "qcm", q: "How old is Louis XIV when he becomes king, in 1643?", c: "Four", w: ["Nine", "Thirteen", "Eighteen"], e: "Born in September 1638, he reigns alone from 1661. During those eighteen years, his mother Anne of Austria and Mazarin govern in his place." },
        { type: "qcm", q: "Which princess does Louis XIV marry in 1660 to seal peace with Spain?", c: "Maria Theresa of Austria, infanta of Spain", w: ["Marie Mancini, niece of Mazarin", "Henrietta of England", "Catherine of Braganza"], e: "The king had loved Marie Mancini, but Mazarin imposes a political marriage on him. The wedding takes place at Saint-Jean-de-Luz, near the border." },
        { type: "tf", q: "Mazarin was born in Italy.", a: true, e: "Born in 1602 at Pescina, in the Abruzzi, he began as a diplomat of the pope before passing into the service of France." },
        { type: "tf", q: "During the Fronde, Louis XIV and his mother flee Paris to take refuge at Versailles.", a: false, e: "The court flees to Saint-Germain-en-Laye in January 1649. Versailles is then only a small hunting lodge." },
        { type: "order", q: "Put these moments of the regency and the Fronde in chronological order.", items: [{ t: "Death of Louis XIII, Anne's regency", y: "1643" }, { t: "The barricades of Paris", y: "1648" }, { t: "Mazarin's return to Paris", y: "1653" }, { t: "Death of Mazarin", y: "1661" }] },
        { type: "match", q: "Match each figure to his or her role in the Fronde.", pairs: [["Anne of Austria", "Regent for her son Louis XIV"], ["Pierre Broussel", "Parlement councillor arrested in 1648"], ["Cardinal de Retz", "Coadjutor of Paris, Fronde leader"], ["Mademoiselle de Montpensier", "Has the Bastille cannon fired"]] },
        { type: "year", q: "In which year is the treaty of the Pyrenees signed between France and Spain?", y: 1659, min: 1600, max: 1700, tol: 1, e: "Signed in November 1659 on Pheasant Island, in the Bidassoa, it ends twenty-four years of war. France gains Roussillon and Artois." }
      ]
    },
    {
      title: "The Sun King and Colbert",
      lesson: {"d":"1661 – 1683","t":"Louis XIV, Fouquet and Colbert","hook":"17 August 1661, Vaux-le-Vicomte: fountains, fireworks, theatre, banquet. The king, guest of honour, says little. A few weeks later, his host is arrested. What happened?","p":"Mazarin is dead. Throughout the kingdom, people wonder who will be the next chief minister. Louis XIV, twenty-two, gathers his ministers and announces, to general astonishment, that there will be none: he will govern himself, surrounded by a small Council. Many think he will soon tire. He will last more than half a century.\n\n## The Party That Cost Fouquet Dear\n\nNicolas Fouquet, superintendent of finances, is then one of the richest men in the kingdom. He has built at Vaux-le-Vicomte a sumptuous château, with the architect Louis Le Vau, the painter Charles Le Brun and the gardener André Le Nôtre. His emblem is a squirrel, with the motto “Quo non ascendet?”, “How high will he not climb?”. In August 1661, he invites the king to a lavish party where Molière's troupe performs a comedy. Louis XIV, who knows the state's coffers are empty, sees in it a provocation, or worse.\n\nOn 5 September, at Nantes, d'Artagnan, captain of the musketeers, arrests Fouquet. The trial lasts three years. Fouquet is sentenced to banishment, but the king worsens the penalty to life imprisonment. He ends his days at Pignerol, in the Alps. As for the Vaux team, Le Vau, Le Brun and Le Nôtre, the king keeps it for himself.\n\n## The King Chooses an Emblem\n\nLouis XIV chooses the sun: a single star that lights all others and gives life. As a teenager, he already danced in court ballets; in 1653, he had appeared as the Rising Sun in the Ballet de la nuit. In 1662, at a great carrousel before the Tuileries, he makes the star his emblem, with a Latin motto, “Nec pluribus impar”, often translated as “not unequal to many” (its exact meaning remains debated). Little by little, the Sun King imposes himself on coins, medals, paintings, tapestries.\n\n## Colbert, the Man of Figures\n\nThe real winner of the affair is Jean-Baptiste Colbert, son of a family of Reims merchants. He had served Mazarin with zeal, managed his fortune, and gathered enough to overwhelm Fouquet. A tireless worker, he becomes controller general of finances in 1665, then secretary of state for the Navy, with the King's Buildings, the arts and commerce. He is credited with the saying that the art of taxation consists in plucking the goose to get the most feathers with the least squawking.\n\nHis doctrine, mercantilism, rests on a simple idea: a country is rich if it sells more than it buys. So one must produce in France and sell abroad. Colbert creates or supports royal manufactories: the Gobelins, for the Crown's tapestries and furniture, under Le Brun's direction, or the mirror manufactory, ancestor of Saint-Gobain, which challenges the mirrors of Venice. He imposes strict quality rules, so that “made in France” is a guarantee of seriousness.\n\nColbert also looks to the sea. He founds the French East India Company for trade with Asia, which will establish trading posts including Pondicherry. He develops the arsenals of Rochefort, Brest and Toulon. He supports the Academy of Sciences in 1666 and the Paris Observatory, where the astronomer Giovanni Domenico Cassini, come from Italy, works. Finally he backs a titanic project, the Canal du Midi, imagined by Pierre-Paul Riquet to link the Atlantic to the Mediterranean. Riquet dies a few months before the opening, but you can still travel his canal by boat.\n\nColbert dies in 1683, worn out and unpopular: taxes weigh heavily, and the king prefers to spend on war and buildings. Yet his work will last: a more orderly administration, a strong navy, prestige manufactories, a taste for “made in France” before the term existed.","fun":["Saint-Mars, Fouquet's jailer at Pignerol, later guarded a mysterious prisoner, who died at the Bastille in 1703 and whose face was hidden: the “Man in the Iron Mask”. Witnesses speak of a velvet mask, it was Voltaire who made it iron, and the man's identity remains debated.","The phrase “L'État, c'est moi” is attributed to Louis XIV, but no serious source reports it in his lifetime. Historians consider it apocryphal: the king rather spoke of his “trade of king”."]},
      items: [
        { type: "qcm", q: "Which superintendent of finances, host of the Vaux-le-Vicomte party, is arrested at Nantes on the king's orders?", c: "Nicolas Fouquet", w: ["Jean-Baptiste Colbert", "Michel Le Tellier", "Hugues de Lionne"], e: "His fortune and his château irritated Louis XIV. Colbert, who had gathered proofs against him, takes his place in the king's trust." },
        { type: "qcm", q: "Which emblem does Louis XIV choose, which earns him his nickname?", c: "The sun", w: ["A crowned lion", "A two-headed eagle", "A Gallic rooster"], e: "He adopts it around 1662, at a great carrousel, with a Latin motto. The sun, a star that lights and gives life to all things, symbolises the king." },
        { type: "qcm", q: "Which minister from a family of Reims merchants runs Louis XIV's Finances and then the Navy?", c: "Jean-Baptiste Colbert", w: ["Louvois", "Nicolas Fouquet", "Hugues de Lionne"], e: "A tireless worker, Colbert also manages buildings, the arts and commerce. Louvois, his rival, handles war." },
        { type: "qcm", q: "Which Parisian royal manufactory makes tapestries and furniture for the Crown?", c: "The Gobelins", w: ["The Sèvres manufactory", "The Baccarat manufactory", "The Saint-Louis crystal works"], e: "Directed by the painter Charles Le Brun, it employs tapestry-makers, goldsmiths and cabinet-makers. The other three manufactories date from the 18th century." },
        { type: "qcm", q: "Which monopoly company does Colbert found to trade with Asia?", c: "The French East India Company", w: ["The Mississippi Company", "The Company of the Holy Sacrament", "The Company of One Hundred Associates"], e: "Created in 1664, it founds trading posts, including Pondicherry in 1674. The Mississippi Company is a much later enterprise, linked to John Law." },
        { type: "tf", q: "The phrase “L'État, c'est moi” attributed to Louis XIV is considered by historians to be apocryphal.", a: true, e: "No serious source reports it in his lifetime. The king spoke of the state as his charge, and in his Memoirs he writes mostly about his duty." },
        { type: "tf", q: "Convicted of embezzlement, Fouquet is executed at the place de Grève.", a: false, e: "The court pronounces banishment, but Louis XIV worsens the penalty to life imprisonment. Fouquet dies in 1680 at Pignerol, in the Alps." },
        { type: "order", q: "Put these decisions of the personal reign in chronological order.", items: [{ t: "Condemnation of Fouquet", y: "1664" }, { t: "Colbert, controller general of finances", y: "1665" }, { t: "Creation of the Academy of Sciences", y: "1666" }, { t: "Colbert, secretary of state for the Navy", y: "1669" }, { t: "Death of Colbert", y: "1683" }] },
        { type: "match", q: "Match each name to its role in the Fouquet affair or the economy.", pairs: [["D'Artagnan", "Arrests Fouquet at Nantes"], ["Pignerol", "Fortress where Fouquet is held"], ["Saint-Gobain", "Mirror and plate-glass manufactory"], ["Pierre-Paul Riquet", "Engineer of the Canal du Midi"]] },
        { type: "year", q: "In which year does Louis XIV announce he will govern alone, without a chief minister?", y: 1661, min: 1600, max: 1700, tol: 1, e: "Mazarin died in March 1661. The king then decides to preside over his Councils himself, which marks the start of his personal reign." }
      ]
    },
    {
      title: "Versailles, a Palace to Govern",
      lesson: {"d":"1623 – 1715","t":"Versailles, a Palace to Govern","hook":"Imagine: you are a duke, rich in lands and titles. To get a pension, an office or a mere glance from the king, you must live at Versailles, in a tiny lodging, far from your estates. Welcome to the court.","p":"At the origin, there is almost nothing: marshes, a village, and a brick hunting lodge that Louis XIII has built in the 1620s, then enlarged into a small château. Louis XIV sees in it a place to his measure. Why Versailles rather than Paris or the Louvre? Probably because Paris is noisy, because he has not forgotten the Fronde, and because he wants a place entirely his own, with no awkward past.\n\n## The Three Magicians\n\nTo build it, the king gathers the team of Vaux-le-Vicomte, Fouquet's: the architect Louis Le Vau, the painter Charles Le Brun and the gardener André Le Nôtre. In May 1664, he gives a festival lasting several days, the “Pleasures of the Enchanted Island”, with ring races, ballets, fireworks, and Molière's troupe, which performs the first acts of Tartuffe. The building site will last decades and mobilise thousands of workers. Le Nôtre designs gardens in perspectives: parterres, basins, groves and the Grand Canal, in the form of a cross, more than a kilometre and a half long, where the king even has gondolas from Venice sail.\n\n## The Hall of Mirrors\n\nFrom 1678, Jules Hardouin-Mansart, grandnephew of the famous François Mansart, takes over the works and gives the château its final face. He replaces the terrace on the garden side with an immense gallery: the Hall of Mirrors is seventy-three metres long, with seventeen arcades of mirrors, 357 mirrors in all, reflecting the light of the garden. At the time, mirrors of this size are an extreme luxury. The ceiling, painted by Le Brun, celebrates the king's exploits. In the park, the king also has a more intimate retreat built, the Grand Trianon, in pink marble.\n\n## A Permanent Theatre\n\nOn 6 May, in the 1680s, the court settles for good, and the government with it. Thousands of people live or lodge at the château: princes, nobles, ministers, servants, cooks, gardeners. The great lords, far from their lands, depend on the king's favours: a pension, an office, a place near him. Being seen at Versailles becomes a matter of survival. It is a clever way of turning turbulent lords into courtiers.\n\nEverything, or nearly, is ceremony. In the morning, at the king's rising, courtiers chosen according to rank attend his waking and dressing, and each has a precise gesture to perform. In the evening, at bedtime, it begins again in reverse. At the grand couvert, the king dines in public before spectators. Who may sit, on what, before whom: privileges are settled to the millimetre. The duke of Saint-Simon, who lives at court and notes everything, left Memoirs where gossip rubs shoulders with great history.\n\n## Water, the Great Problem\n\nVersailles has no great river at its feet. To feed the fountains of the park, water must be brought from afar. Ponds and aqueducts are laid out, and the famous Marly machine is built, a series of wheels and pumps installed on the Seine to lift water up to the château. Water is still not enough to make all the jets spring up at once: according to tradition, fountain-keepers passed the word along in the groves so as to open each jet only when the king arrived.\n\nLouis XIV dies at Versailles in 1715, and his successors will reside there until the Revolution. The palace has since seen decisive moments: in 1871, the German Empire is proclaimed in the Hall of Mirrors; in 1919, the treaty ending the First World War is signed there. A UNESCO World Heritage site, it attracts millions of visitors today.","fun":["The 357 mirrors of the Hall of Mirrors are not single large panes: each of the seventeen arcades has twenty-one, assembled. These glasses came from the royal manufactory Colbert had launched to rival Venice.","Saint-Simon writes that, thanks to an almanac and a watch, one could know three hundred leagues from the court what Louis XIV was doing at every hour of the day, so regulated were his days."]},
      items: [
        { type: "qcm", q: "Which gardener and designer creates the French-style gardens of Versailles?", c: "André Le Nôtre", w: ["Louis Le Vau", "Jules Hardouin-Mansart", "Charles Le Brun"], e: "Le Nôtre had already worked for Fouquet at Vaux-le-Vicomte. At Versailles, he lines up basins, parterres and perspectives over hundreds of hectares." },
        { type: "qcm", q: "Which architect designs the Hall of Mirrors and the royal chapel of Versailles?", c: "Jules Hardouin-Mansart", w: ["Louis Le Vau", "Claude Perrault", "François Mansart"], e: "Grandnephew of François Mansart, he becomes the king's first architect. Le Vau, first architect of the château, had died in 1670." },
        { type: "qcm", q: "Which first painter to the king directs the decoration of Versailles and paints the ceilings of the Hall of Mirrors?", c: "Charles Le Brun", w: ["Nicolas Poussin", "Hyacinthe Rigaud", "Antoine Watteau"], e: "The ceilings of the gallery tell the victories of Louis XIV. Rigaud is mostly known for his portrait of the king, and Watteau belongs to the next generation." },
        { type: "qcm", q: "Under Louis XIII, what is the Versailles site first used for?", c: "Hunting, with a meeting lodge", w: ["Defending the road to Paris", "Housing a royal abbey", "Making tapestries"], e: "Louis XIII had a lodge built there from 1623, then a small château of brick and stone. Louis XIV would transform it from the 1660s." },
        { type: "qcm", q: "What is the morning ceremony called in which the king is woken and dressed before the courtiers?", c: "The king's rising (lever)", w: ["The grand couvert", "The king's bedtime (coucher)", "The carrousel"], e: "Each person has a precise rank in this ceremony, and being admitted near the king is a much sought honour. The grand couvert is the king's public meal." },
        { type: "tf", q: "The German Empire was proclaimed in 1871 in the Hall of Mirrors at Versailles.", a: true, e: "The king of Prussia was proclaimed emperor at Versailles on 18 January 1871, while the German army was still besieging Paris. France experienced it as a humiliation." },
        { type: "tf", q: "The court settles permanently at Versailles as early as 1661, at the start of Louis XIV's personal reign.", a: false, e: "In 1661, the enlargement works are only just beginning. Only in 1682 does the king fix his court and government there." },
        { type: "order", q: "Put these moments in the history of Versailles in chronological order.", items: [{ t: "Louis XIII has a hunting lodge built", y: "1623" }, { t: "Festival of the Enchanted Island", y: "1664" }, { t: "Hardouin-Mansart enlarges the château", y: "1678" }, { t: "Hall of Mirrors completed", y: "1684" }, { t: "The royal chapel is completed", y: "1710" }] },
        { type: "match", q: "Match each name to its place in the world of Versailles.", pairs: [["Louis Le Vau", "First architect of Louis XIV"], ["Saint-Simon", "Duke and memoirist of the court"], ["Marly machine", "Pumps that feed the fountains"], ["Grand Trianon", "Pink marble palace, the king's retreat"]] },
        { type: "year", q: "In which year does Louis XIV install the court and government at Versailles?", y: 1682, min: 1620, max: 1720, tol: 1, e: "On 6 May 1682, the king settles at Versailles. He stays there until his death and his successors will remain in this palace until 1789." }
      ]
    },
    {
      title: "Molière, Racine and Lully",
      lesson: {"d":"1637 – 1697","t":"Molière, Racine, La Fontaine and Lully","hook":"17 February 1673, Palais-Royal theatre. The Imaginary Invalid coughs and whines, the audience roars with laughter. But the actor playing the part is truly ill. He will finish the play, and die a few hours later.","p":"To make Louis XIV laugh, you need an exceptional man. Jean-Baptiste Poquelin, born in Paris in 1622 into a family of the king's upholsterers, could have taken over his father's post. At twenty-one, he chooses the theatre, founds with the Béjart family the Illustre Théâtre and takes the name Molière. The troupe goes bankrupt, and he goes off for a dozen years on the roads of the provinces. Back in Paris in 1658, he performs before the king, who lends him a hall.\n\n## Laughing at Everything, or Nearly\n\nHis comedies target eternal faults: avarice in The Miser, the vanity of bourgeois dreaming of nobility in The Bourgeois Gentleman, Alceste's intransigence in The Misanthrope, the fear of illness in The Imaginary Invalid. With the musician Lully, he invents the comedy-ballet, which mixes theatre, music and dance to entertain the king.\n\nBut it is with Tartuffe that he causes scandal. This comedy of a false devotee who manipulates a family is banned after its first performances: devout people see in it a mockery of religion. Molière must fight for five years before he can stage it again. He targets those who use piety to deceive, not faith itself. The word “tartuffe” has entered the language to mean a hypocrite.\n\n## Corneille, Racine: Tragedy\n\nWhile Molière makes people laugh, tragedy makes them weep. Pierre Corneille, born in Rouen, triumphs in 1637 with Le Cid, then writes Horace, Cinna, Polyeucte: his heroes are torn between honour and love. Jean Racine, younger, an orphan raised at Port-Royal, prefers to explore the passions: Andromaque, Britannicus, Phèdre. The verse is chiselled, the language limpid. In classical theatre, the three unities are respected: a single action, in a single place, within twenty-four hours.\n\n## Fables, Tales and Letters\n\nJean de La Fontaine, born at Château-Thierry, publishes from 1668 his Fables, in verse of varied lengths, inspired by Aesop. The fox, the crow, the grasshopper, the ant speak there like courtiers. “The reason of the strongest is always the best,” says the wolf to the lamb. Boileau fixes the rules of good taste in L'Art poétique, with the celebrated advice that what is well conceived is clearly stated. Jean de La Bruyère, for his part, paints in Les Caractères the foibles of his contemporaries.\n\nWomen of letters shine too. Madame de Sévigné writes to her daughter, Madame de Grignan, lively and funny letters that tell the life of the time. Madame de La Fayette publishes La Princesse de Clèves, often considered one of the first psychological novels. At the end of the century, Charles Perrault shapes popular tales: Cinderella, Little Red Riding Hood, Sleeping Beauty, Puss in Boots.\n\n## Lully, the Florentine\n\nIn music, the master is Jean-Baptiste Lully, born in Florence in 1632 and arrived in France as a teenager. A dancer, violinist, shrewd courtier, naturalised French, he becomes superintendent of the king's music and in 1672 obtains the privilege of the Opera: he creates tragedy in music, which mixes song, orchestra and ballet. He then falls out with Molière, with whom he had collaborated. In January 1687, while conducting a Te Deum, he strikes his foot with the big cane he beats time with. The wound becomes infected, and he dies before the end of March.\n\nBack to Molière, whose end fits into one evening. Ill in the lungs, he plays on 17 February 1673 the fourth performance of The Imaginary Invalid, finishes the play as best he can, goes home and dies in the evening. At that time, the Church refuses the sacraments to actors who have not renounced their profession, and the burial poses a problem. With the king's agreement, Molière is buried at night, without a solemn service. A few years later, Louis XIV brings the Paris troupes together: the Comédie-Française, heir to Molière's troupe, is born. It is still called “the house of Molière”.","fun":["Molière was never a member of the French Academy, which has become a legend: people speak of the “forty-first armchair” to evoke the great writers who never sat there.","People often say “the language of Molière” to mean French. And the Molières ceremony, created in 1987, rewards French theatre every year: an author dead for over three centuries still gives his name to the trophies."]},
      items: [
        { type: "qcm", q: "What is Molière's real name?", c: "Jean-Baptiste Poquelin", w: ["Jean-Baptiste Lully", "Jean-Baptiste Colbert", "Jean de La Fontaine"], e: "The son of a king's upholsterer, born in Paris in 1622, he takes the stage name Molière when he starts out with his troupe, the Illustre Théâtre." },
        { type: "qcm", q: "Which Molière comedy, featuring a false devotee, is banned by the authorities after its first performances?", c: "Tartuffe", w: ["The Misanthrope", "The Miser", "The Bourgeois Gentleman"], e: "The devout see in it an attack on religion. Backed by the king, the play will be allowed to the public only in 1669." },
        { type: "qcm", q: "Which tragedy by Racine features the wife of Theseus, consumed by a forbidden passion?", c: "Phèdre", w: ["Andromaque", "Britannicus", "Athalie"], e: "Inspired by Euripides, Phèdre (1677) is one of his masterpieces. Racine then stopped writing for the secular theatre for more than ten years." },
        { type: "qcm", q: "Which author publishes in verse fables inspired by Aesop, like “The Grasshopper and the Ant”?", c: "Jean de La Fontaine", w: ["Charles Perrault", "Nicolas Boileau", "Jean de La Bruyère"], e: "Born at Château-Thierry, La Fontaine was also protected by Fouquet. Perrault is mostly known for his tales in prose." },
        { type: "qcm", q: "Which composer of Italian origin directs the king's music and founds French opera?", c: "Jean-Baptiste Lully", w: ["Jean-Philippe Rameau", "Marc-Antoine Charpentier", "François Couperin"], e: "Born in Florence, become superintendent of the king's music, he receives in 1672 the privilege of the Opera. Rameau belongs to the 18th century, Charpentier and Couperin were French." },
        { type: "tf", q: "Lully died of an infection after hurting his foot with his conductor's cane.", a: true, e: "He was striking the floor to beat time for a Te Deum when the cane pierced his foot. Gangrene set in and he died in March 1687." },
        { type: "tf", q: "La Fontaine's Fables are written in prose, with no rhyme at all.", a: false, e: "They are written in verse of varied lengths, with rhymes, which gives each fable a lively rhythm that is easy to remember." },
        { type: "order", q: "Put these famous works and deaths in chronological order.", items: [{ t: "Premiere of Corneille's Le Cid", y: "1637" }, { t: "First books of La Fontaine's Fables", y: "1668" }, { t: "Death of Molière", y: "1673" }, { t: "Death of Lully", y: "1687" }, { t: "Perrault's Tales", y: "1697" }] },
        { type: "match", q: "Match each author to his or her work.", pairs: [["Pierre Corneille", "Horace, Cinna, Polyeucte"], ["Nicolas Boileau", "L'Art poétique"], ["Jean de La Bruyère", "Les Caractères"], ["Madame de Sévigné", "Letters to her daughter"]] },
        { type: "year", q: "In which year does Louis XIV bring the Paris theatre troupes together to create the Comédie-Française?", y: 1680, min: 1620, max: 1720, tol: 1, e: "In October 1680, the troupe of the Hôtel Guénégaud, heir to Molière's, merges with that of the Hôtel de Bourgogne. It is the oldest theatre company still active." }
      ]
    },
    {
      title: "Vauban and the King's Wars",
      lesson: {"d":"1667 – 1714","t":"Vauban and the Sun King's Wars","hook":"Some fifty sieges led, hundreds of fortified places, countless leagues on horseback: Vauban spent his life on the roads of the kingdom. Yet this soldier worried above all about the fate of the poor.","p":"Louis XIV dreams of glory, and glory is won in war. But there is also a very concrete concern: the kingdom is vulnerable in the north, where there is no natural frontier and from which enemy armies have several times threatened Paris. With his secretary of state for War, Louvois, the king builds the greatest army in Europe, better fed, better paid, better disciplined.\n\n## Repeated Wars\n\nThe conflicts follow one another. The War of Devolution (1667-1668), over Queen Maria Theresa's rights to territories of the Spanish Netherlands, brings places in Flanders. The Dutch War (1672-1678) targets the United Provinces, rich and awkward for French trade; it ends with Franche-Comté. In 1688 the War of the League of Augsburg breaks out: against France now stand the Empire, Spain, England, the United Provinces. It lasts nine years. At sea, Tourville's fleet is beaten at La Hougue in 1692. In Germany, the French army ravages the Palatinate and burns Heidelberg, leaving a bitter memory in Europe.\n\n## Vauban, Genius of Stones\n\nSébastien Le Prestre de Vauban is born in 1633 in the Morvan, into a minor nobility without fortune. Young, he fights in Condé's camp during the Fronde, is taken prisoner, then passes into the king's service, who will never regret it. An engineer of genius, he becomes commissary general of fortifications, and marshal of France at the end of his life. He perfects the art of attacking places: instead of hurling oneself against the walls, one digs zigzag trenches, the “parallels”, which bring the attackers gradually closer under cover from fire, and uses ricochet fire to sweep the ramparts. He also knows how to defend: his bastioned places, star-shaped, leave no blind angle.\n\nIn 1673, Vauban writes to Louvois that it would be good for the king to think of making his “pré carré”: a tightened frontier, protected by a double line of strongholds, rather than a scattered kingdom. He builds or modernises Lille, Besançon, Briançon, Neuf-Brisach, a new town on an octagonal plan. Twelve groups of fortifications designed by him have been on the UNESCO list since 2008. In Paris, Louis XIV also founds the Hôtel des Invalides, to house soldiers wounded or too old, a gesture that shows a king concerned for those who fight for him.\n\n## The Marshal and the Poor\n\nVauban travels the kingdom and sees the countryside growing poorer. In his “Project for a Royal Tithe”, he proposes a tax proportional to income, paid by all, including the nobility and clergy, who were largely exempt. The king has the book banned, and Vauban, fallen into disgrace, dies soon after, at seventy-three.\n\n## The Last War, the Hardest\n\nAt the end of the century, the king of Spain Charles II, who has no child, dies after bequeathing his immense empire to Philip of Anjou, grandson of Louis XIV. To accept is to risk seeing France and Spain one day united; to refuse is to offer this inheritance to the Austrian Habsburgs. Louis XIV accepts. Europe forms a coalition. Marlborough, the English general, and Prince Eugene of Savoy, born in Paris but whom Louis XIV had not wanted in his army, win great victories, notably at Blenheim, in 1704.\n\nThe winter of 1709 is one of the most terrible of the reign: the cold freezes the rivers, the crops perish, famine claims hundreds of thousands of victims. The Allies set conditions the king finds unacceptable, among them that he himself drive his grandson from the throne of Spain: Louis XIV then addresses a letter to his peoples to explain why the war goes on. The recovery comes in 1712: Marshal de Villars surprises the allied army at Denain and beats it. The treaties of Utrecht, in 1713, end the war: Philip V remains king of Spain, but renounces the crown of France, and England obtains among other things Gibraltar.","fun":["The song “Malbrough s'en va-t-en guerre” was born, it is said, from a rumour announcing the death of Marlborough after the battle of Malplaquet. He was not dead, but the song has survived to our day.","Neuf-Brisach was founded in 1698 to replace Vieux-Brisach, which France had to return to the Empire. Seen from the sky, its regular octagonal plan is a model of Vauban's art."]},
      items: [
        { type: "qcm", q: "Which military engineer, become marshal of France, builds the belt of fortresses of the “pré carré”?", c: "Vauban", w: ["Louvois", "Turenne", "Villars"], e: "Sébastien Le Prestre de Vauban led some fifty sieges and had hundreds of places fortified. Twelve groups of fortifications designed by him have been UNESCO-listed since 2008." },
        { type: "qcm", q: "Which grandson of Louis XIV becomes king of Spain, triggering a European war?", c: "Philip of Anjou, become Philip V", w: ["Louis, duke of Burgundy", "Charles, duke of Berry", "Philip, duke of Orléans"], e: "Charles II of Spain, childless, had bequeathed his throne to Philip. He founds the dynasty of the Spanish Bourbons, still on the throne today." },
        { type: "qcm", q: "Which city of the United Provinces gives its name to the treaties of 1713 ending the War of the Spanish Succession?", c: "Utrecht", w: ["Nijmegen", "Rijswijk", "Amsterdam"], e: "Philip V keeps Spain, but renounces any claim to the crown of France. Nijmegen and Rijswijk are the cities of older treaties, in 1678 and 1697." },
        { type: "qcm", q: "Which battle of 1712, won by Marshal de Villars, restores France's situation?", c: "Denain", w: ["Malplaquet", "Fleurus", "Rocroi"], e: "The marshal surprises part of the allied army on the Scheldt. Malplaquet (1709) was a very bloody battle that the French lost." },
        { type: "qcm", q: "Which secretary of state for War, son of Michel Le Tellier, modernises Louis XIV's army?", c: "The marquis de Louvois", w: ["Jean-Baptiste Colbert", "Nicolas Fouquet", "Hugues de Lionne"], e: "He organises supply, discipline and troop depots. His authority over the army makes him one of the most powerful men in the kingdom." },
        { type: "tf", q: "Vauban proposes establishing a tax that would also reach the nobility and the clergy.", a: true, e: "In his “Project for a Royal Tithe”, published in 1707, he asks for a tax proportional to everyone's income. The book is banned, and Vauban dies the same year." },
        { type: "tf", q: "The War of the Spanish Succession ends with Spain and France united under a single king.", a: false, e: "The European powers want to avoid this risk: Philip V keeps the throne of Spain, but renounces for himself and his descendants the crown of France." },
        { type: "order", q: "Put these wars and battles in chronological order.", items: [{ t: "War of Devolution", y: "1667" }, { t: "Dutch War", y: "1672" }, { t: "Start of the War of the League of Augsburg", y: "1688" }, { t: "Naval defeat of La Hougue", y: "1692" }, { t: "Start of the War of the Spanish Succession", y: "1701" }] },
        { type: "match", q: "Match each name to its role in the king's wars.", pairs: [["Marlborough", "English general, victor at Blenheim"], ["Prince Eugene", "General in the emperor's service"], ["Neuf-Brisach", "Stronghold built by Vauban"], ["Hôtel des Invalides", "Home for old wounded soldiers"]] },
        { type: "year", q: "In which year does Charles II of Spain die, whose succession triggers a European war?", y: 1700, min: 1640, max: 1730, tol: 1, e: "The king of Spain dies on 1 November 1700, childless. His will names Philip of Anjou, which draws Europe into war." }
      ]
    },
    {
      title: "Faith, Conscience and Twilight",
      lesson: {"d":"1656 – 1715","t":"Revocation, Jansenism and the King's Death","hook":"A king wants all his subjects to pray the same way. Families must choose between their conscience and their homeland. How was the end of Louis XIV's reign lived by those who believed differently?","p":"Over the course of the reign, Louis XIV has become convinced that a king must have one people and one faith. After a brilliant youth, he becomes a more pious king, under the influence of Madame de Maintenon, widow of the poet Scarron and former governess of his children. Granddaughter of Agrippa d'Aubigné, a Protestant poet, she herself was raised in the Reformed faith before converting. The king marries her in secret, probably after the death of Queen Maria Theresa. In 1686, she founds the house of Saint-Cyr, which offers an education to young noble girls without fortune.\n\n## The Edict of Nantes Revoked\n\nIn the 1680s, pressure on Protestants increases: temples are closed, certain trades are forbidden them, and soldiers are billeted in their homes, the dragonnades, to push them to convert. The king persuades himself that conversion is massive and that the Edict of Nantes no longer has a purpose. In October 1685, the edict of Fontainebleau revokes it: Protestant worship is banned, temples are destroyed, pastors have fifteen days to leave the kingdom, while the faithful have no right to leave.\n\n## The Exile of the Huguenots\n\nYet tens of thousands of Protestants leave France, by dangerous paths, to Switzerland, the United Provinces, England or Prussia, whose elector of Brandenburg invites them through the Edict of Potsdam. By estimates, they number between 150,000 and 200,000. They take with them know-how and trade: silk, watchmaking, banking. Those who stay live discreetly and meet in secret; their assemblies will be called “the Desert”. In the Tour de Constance, at Aigues-Mortes, Protestant women are imprisoned for years; the most famous, Marie Durand, locked up in 1730, stays there thirty-eight years, until 1768.\n\nIn 1702, in the Cévennes, Protestants pushed to the limit rise up: these are the Camisards. The revolt breaks out when they kill at Pont-de-Montvert the abbé du Chayla, an archpriest who had had Protestants imprisoned. For several years, poorly armed peasants, led by chiefs like Jean Cavalier, a former shepherd turned war leader at twenty, hold out against the royal armies. The repression is harsh.\n\n## Port-Royal and Pascal's Genius\n\nLouis XIV also goes after the Jansenists. This current of Catholicism, born of the ideas of the bishop of Ypres Jansenius, insists on God's grace and on a demanding morality. Its hearth is the abbey of Port-Royal, near Paris. The authorities and the Jesuits distrust it, fearing a party of dissidents. The nuns are expelled from Port-Royal des Champs, and in 1710 the buildings are razed.\n\nPort-Royal had counted Blaise Pascal among its friends. A precocious genius, he invents at nineteen a calculating machine, the Pascaline, to help his father, a tax collector in Rouen, and studies the vacuum and pressure. On the night of 23 November 1654, he has an intense religious experience that he notes on a paper sewn into the lining of his coat. In 1656, he defends his friends in the Provincial Letters, stinging letters against the Jesuits. He dies at thirty-nine, leaving notes on faith published under the title Pensées.\n\n## Bossuet, Fénelon and the Evening of the Reign\n\nAt court, two great bishops write. Bossuet, bishop of Meaux, an immense orator, delivers famous funeral orations and tutors the Dauphin, the king's son; he defends the idea of a monarchy by divine right. Fénelon, archbishop of Cambrai, educates the king's grandson, the duke of Burgundy, and writes The Adventures of Telemachus, a novel in which readers find a critique of absolute power.\n\nThe last years are dark: wars, bereavements, with the death of his son and then of his grandson, the duke of Burgundy, heir to the throne. Louis XIV dies at Versailles on 1 September 1715, after seventy-two years of reign, of gangrene in the leg. His great-grandson, Louis XV, is five. It is said the king declared: “I am going, but the state will always remain.” He leaves a powerful but exhausted country, and a language and culture that shine over Europe.","fun":["Pascal gave his name to the unit of pressure, the pascal, found on weather maps, with hectopascals. A 17th-century mathematician thus invites himself into our daily bulletins.","The abbey of Port-Royal des Champs was razed, but its ruins and a museum can be visited today at Magny-les-Hameaux, near Versailles: you can gauge the place it held in the religious life of the century."]},
      items: [
        { type: "qcm", q: "Which edict, signed by Louis XIV in 1685, ends the Edict of Nantes?", c: "The edict of Fontainebleau", w: ["The edict of Marly", "The edict of Compiègne", "The edict of Saint-Germain"], e: "Signed at Fontainebleau, it bans Protestant worship in France. Louis XIV thinks he has achieved religious unity, but he deprives the country of thousands of craftsmen and merchants." },
        { type: "qcm", q: "Which mathematician and Christian thinker, close to Port-Royal, is the author of the Pensées?", c: "Blaise Pascal", w: ["René Descartes", "Nicolas Malebranche", "François de La Rochefoucauld"], e: "Pascal also invents a calculating machine and studies the vacuum. His Pensées, published after his death, were left unfinished." },
        { type: "qcm", q: "Which bishop of Meaux, great orator of funeral orations, is tutor to the Dauphin, son of Louis XIV?", c: "Jacques-Bénigne Bossuet", w: ["François de Fénelon", "Jean-François Paul de Gondi", "Jean-Baptiste Massillon"], e: "Fénelon, archbishop of Cambrai, was the tutor of the king's grandson, the duke of Burgundy. Bossuet defends monarchy by divine right." },
        { type: "qcm", q: "What fate does Louis XIV reserve for the abbey of Port-Royal des Champs, hearth of Jansenism?", c: "The nuns are expelled, the abbey is razed", w: ["It becomes a royal residence", "It is given to the Protestants", "It is turned into barracks"], e: "The last buildings are demolished in 1710, and even the bodies buried in the cemetery are moved. The king wanted to erase this symbol of dissent." },
        { type: "qcm", q: "Who becomes king of France at the death of Louis XIV, his great-grandson?", c: "Louis XV", w: ["Louis XVI", "Philip V of Spain", "Louis, duke of Burgundy"], e: "The duke of Burgundy, father of Louis XV, had died in 1712. The child king is placed under the regency of Philip of Orléans." },
        { type: "tf", q: "After the revocation of the Edict of Nantes, tens of thousands of Protestants leave France.", a: true, e: "Despite the ban on leaving, many flee to England, the United Provinces, Switzerland or Germany. They take their know-how with them." },
        { type: "tf", q: "Blaise Pascal died very old, at more than eighty.", a: false, e: "Pascal died in 1662, at 39, after a life of fragile health. Yet he marked mathematics, physics and literature alike." },
        { type: "order", q: "Put these religious and personal events in chronological order.", items: [{ t: "Start of Pascal's Provincial Letters", y: "1656" }, { t: "Death of Pascal", y: "1662" }, { t: "Revocation of the Edict of Nantes", y: "1685" }, { t: "Destruction of Port-Royal des Champs", y: "1710" }] },
        { type: "match", q: "Match each figure of the end of the reign to the description.", pairs: [["Madame de Maintenon", "Secret wife of the king, founds Saint-Cyr"], ["Camisards", "Revolted Protestants of the Cévennes"], ["Jansenius", "Bishop of Ypres, author of the Augustinus"], ["Fénelon", "Archbishop of Cambrai, wrote Telemachus"]] },
        { type: "year", q: "In which year does Louis XIV die, after seventy-two years of reign?", y: 1715, min: 1650, max: 1740, tol: 1, e: "The king dies at Versailles on 1 September 1715, a few days before his seventy-seventh birthday. His reign, from 1643 to 1715, is the longest in French history." }
      ]
    }
  ]
};
